.TH "GLESHA" "1" "" "glesha" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH NAME
glesha \- encrypted archives and incremental cloud backups
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBglesha\fR [global options] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR] [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBglesha\fR creates encrypted, compressed tar archives and keeps histories of them in Amazon S3 and Backblaze B2 buckets. Compression, encryption, and transfers are done internally; no external programs are needed. Full archives are OpenPGP messages that \fBgpg\fR(1) and \fBtar\fR(1) can also read.
.PP
A \fIset\fR is a named group of source directories, called \fIroots\fR. Each \fBglesha run\fR adds a \fIsnapshot\fR: a full archive, or an incremental one that holds the changes since the \fIbaseline\fR. A \fIremote\fR is a configured bucket. The \fIcatalog\fR of a set records its snapshots and files; it is kept in local state and, unless the set is local\-only, encrypted on a remote so that other computers can find it.
.PP
Sets and snapshots have UUIDs. A set can be named by its name or its UUID.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Backup sets
.TP
.BR glesha\-create (1)
Register a new backup set.
.TP
.BR glesha\-configure (1)
Change the settings of a set.
.TP
.BR glesha\-run (1)
Create a snapshot and upload it.
.TP
.BR glesha\-retry (1)
Resume the pending operation of a set.
.TP
.BR glesha\-restore (1)
Restore a snapshot into a directory.
.TP
.BR glesha\-import (1)
Add an existing full archive to a set.
.TP
.BR glesha\-move (1)
Copy the history of a set to another remote.
.TP
.BR glesha\-storage\-class (1)
Change the AWS storage class of a snapshot.
.TP
.BR glesha\-cleanup (1)
Delete local archive copies that are stored remotely.
.SS Inspection
.TP
.BR glesha\-list (1)
List known backup sets.
.TP
.BR glesha\-status (1)
Show the state, size, and cost of backup sets.
.TP
.BR glesha\-history (1)
List the snapshots of a set.
.TP
.BR glesha\-browse (1)
List the files in a snapshot.
.SS Catalogs
.TP
.BR glesha\-catalog (1)
Discover, open, and synchronize catalogs: \fBsync\fR, \fBunlock\fR, \fBresolve\fR, \fBpull\fR, \fBpush\fR, \fBreconcile\fR, and \fBabandon\fR.
.SS Standalone files
.TP
.BR glesha\-archive (1)
Create a standalone encrypted archive.
.TP
.BR glesha\-decrypt (1)
Decrypt an archive to a compressed tar file.
.TP
.BR glesha\-extract (1)
Unpack an archive into a new directory.
.TP
.BR glesha\-upload (1)
Upload a file to remote buckets.
.TP
.BR glesha\-download (1)
Download an object from remote buckets.
.TP
.BR glesha\-check (1)
Test access to remote buckets.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR glesha\-state (1)
Move local state from the legacy location.
.TP
.BR glesha\-help (1)
Show help for a command.
.TP
.BR glesha\-version (1)
Show version information.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are accepted by every command, before or after the command name. Command options must follow the command name.
.TP
.B \-\-config FILE
Read settings from FILE instead of USER_CONFIG/glesha/config.toml. A missing default file is not an error.
.TP
.B \-\-state\-dir DIR
Keep local state in DIR. Overrides the [catalog] directory setting.
.TP
.B \-\-env FILE
Load credentials from FILE. Each line is NAME=VALUE; blank lines, # comments, and an export prefix are allowed. Variables already set in the environment take precedence.
.TP
.B \-\-format FORMAT
Output format: plain or json (default: plain). \fBglesha status\fR also accepts table and csv. See OUTPUT.
.TP
.B \-\-progress MODE
Progress on stderr: auto, plain, or off (default: auto). auto draws a progress bar on a terminal and prints one line per step otherwise; plain always prints lines.
.TP
.B \-\-color WHEN
Color: auto, yes, or no (default: auto). always and never are accepted as aliases.
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-log\-level LEVEL
Log level: debug, info, warn, error, or silent (default: info). warn and above also hide progress.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help for the command.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show version information.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from a TOML file. Remotes are defined in the [aws] and [b2] tables, and in [remotes.NAME] tables with a kind of aws or b2; the table name is the remote name used with \-\-to and \-\-from. Other tables set defaults for [archive], [memory], [workers], [backup], [catalog], and [cold].
.PP
Command options take precedence over the configuration file, which takes precedence over built\-in defaults. Defaults shown in command help are the built\-in ones.
.SH BUCKET ACCESS
Without \-\-from or \-\-to, a command may access every remote it needs: the set's remotes, its catalog remote, and the configured remotes that hold registry copies. If either option is given, only the remotes they name are accessed, and a command that needs another remote fails. Naming remotes does not make a command read\-only.
.SH PASSPHRASES
The catalog passphrase and archive passphrases are separate. Catalog metadata can be downloaded without a passphrase; reading or changing a catalog needs it. Restoring needs the archive passphrase of every snapshot involved.
.PP
Passphrases are read from a hidden prompt, or from a file given with \-\-passphrase\-file or \-\-catalog\-passphrase\-file, where \- means stdin. The catalog passphrase can also be set in GLESHA_CATALOG_PASSPHRASE. Passphrases are never stored. Without a terminal, a missing passphrase is an error.
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to stdout. Progress, prompts, warnings, and errors go to stderr. A '\-' in human output marks an unknown value. Human output is ASCII and may change between versions.
.PP
With \-\-format json, stdout carries one JSON record per line, each of the form {"version", "kind", "data"}. Catalog sync records have version 3; other results have version 2. Listings print one record per item and none when empty. stderr carries version 1 records whose kind is progress, error, warning, info, or debug. Neither stream contains color codes. Error records include a code, a message, and the exit status.
.PP
JSON output does not approve anything. Prompts still appear on stderr and fail without a terminal; use passphrase files, \-\-assume\-yes, and \-\-request\-retrieval for unattended use.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS credentials. AWS_ACCESS_KEY and AWS_SECRET_KEY are also accepted.
.TP
.B AWS_BUCKET_NAME, AWS_REGION, AWS_STORAGE_CLASS
Used for the aws remote when not set in the configuration file.
.TP
.B B2_KEY_ID, B2_APPLICATION_KEY
Backblaze B2 credentials.
.TP
.B B2_BUCKET_NAME, B2_BUCKET_ENDPOINT
Used for the b2 remote when not set in the configuration file.
.TP
.B GLESHA_CATALOG_PASSPHRASE
Catalog passphrase.
.TP
.B TERM
If dumb, progress is printed as lines.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I USER_CONFIG/glesha/config.toml
Default configuration file. USER_CONFIG is ~/.config on Linux, ~/Library/Application Support on macOS, and %AppData% on Windows.
.TP
.I DATA/glesha/state
Default local state. DATA is $XDG_DATA_HOME or ~/.local/share on Linux, ~/Library/Application Support on macOS, and %LocalAppData% on Windows.
.TP
.I USER_CONFIG/glesha/state\-v2
Legacy local state; see \fBglesha\-state\fR(1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, or an unknown set.
.TP
.B 3
Pending work must be finished first.
.TP
.B 4
The local and remote catalogs conflict.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Back up a directory to Backblaze B2 and restore it elsewhere:
.PP
.nf
glesha create docs ~/Documents \-\-to b2
glesha run docs \-\-env ~/.config/glesha/cloud.env
glesha run docs \-\-incremental
glesha history docs
glesha restore docs \-\-into ~/recovered
.fi
.PP
Find and open the same set on another computer:
.PP
.nf
glesha catalog sync \-\-from b2
glesha list
glesha catalog unlock ID
glesha configure docs \-\-root Documents=~/Documents
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpg (1),
.BR tar (1)
